prompt:
You are contributing to TaskForge, a TypeScript + Bun CLI used in the RazorCascade memory reliability study.

Execution mode: cascade
Standardized task: 3. List, filter, and view tasks
Objective: Support querying tasks by status, priority, tags, and free-text search.

Context from prior work:
Goal: Define a typed Task data model and add safe JSON-backed persistence so TaskForge can create, read, update, and list tasks from the Bun CLI.
Decisions: Use Bun runtime and simple Bun.argv-driven CLI surface (minimal parsing: flags, positionals, --help/-h, --version/-v).; Persist tasks as a versioned envelope { version: 1, tasks: Task[] } in a single JSON file (.taskforge/tasks.json).; Validate JSON at runtime with lightweight guards (isTask/isTaskArray) rather than trusting TypeScript at runtime.; Perform atomic-ish saves by writing a temp file in the same directory then renaming over the target file.; Expose a thin store API: loadTasks, saveTasks, getTaskById, upsertTask, listTasks to keep CLI handlers modular.
Risks: Concurrent writes: two CLI invocations may clobber each other—no locking implemented yet.; Portability: Bun-specific argv/runtime choices and reading package.json at runtime may complicate future Node packaging.; Schema drift: persisted JSON may diverge from TypeScript types; guards/tests must be kept in sync to avoid silent corruption.
Snippets: const STORAGE_PATH = '.taskforge/tasks.json'; | export type TaskStatus = 'pending' | 'running' | 'completed' | 'failed';
export interface Task { id: string; title: string; status: TaskStatus; createdAt: string; updatedAt: string; description?: string; } | const envelope = { version: 1, tasks: tasksArray };
// atomic-ish save:
// 1) write JSON to .taskforge/.tmp-<rand>
// 2) flush/close
// 3) rename tmp -> tasks.json (same dir rename minimizes partial-write risk)
Invariants: file-not-found => return empty store; invalid JSON / invalid shape => fail with clear error instead of silently corrupting state; schema version = 1; storage file = .taskforge/tasks.json

Produce a concise engineering update that includes:
1. Goal
2. Proposed implementation details
3. Validation or testing
4. Risks
